# Call-site ledger of funcy at 04aafae9a7390cce610b45b5dca3a630e43c1713.
# Hand-written from the source; judgements in funcy.md.
default reject funcy.calc.CacheMemory.__getitem__: no CacheMemory is subscripted, iterated, unpacked, searched or converted at this site; CacheMemory is made only by cache() and lives in the closure of _memory_decorator, whose wrapper subscripts it (calc.py:52, accepted there); the operands here are dicts, lists, tuples, strs, sets, sys.modules, code objects, iterators or client collections, and handing wrapper.memory to another function is an arbitrary package object where any object is accepted (B3); dict.get, dict.pop and dict iteration on a CacheMemory do not call __getitem__
default reject funcy.calc.CacheMemory.__setitem__: no CacheMemory is the target of this item assignment; the only CacheMemory store is memory[key] = ... in _memory_decorator's wrapper (calc.py:55, accepted there); the targets here are dicts, lists, instance __dict__s or client collections (B3)
default reject funcy.decorators.Call.__getattr__: Call.__getattr__ runs only for an attribute that a Call instance lacks; the package reads only _func, _args, _kwargs and __dict__ of a Call, which its __init__ sets or the type provides, and at the other sites the receiver is not a Call (a module, a function, a class, a str, a dict, a code object, a regex, a partial, a client object)
default reject funcy.objects.LazyObject.__getattr__: no receiver of this attribute load is a LazyObject; the package never makes one (LazyObject is a public class that only clients instantiate), and passing one to another funcy function is an arbitrary package object where any object is accepted (B3); inside LazyObject, _init and _setup are found without __getattr__, and after _setup the instance has the class of the made object
default reject funcy.objects.LazyObject.__setattr__: no target of this attribute store is a LazyObject (the package never makes one; B3); the stores are on functions, wrappers, Call, CacheMemory and context-manager instances, a cls passed to monkey, and the inner functions of the decorators
default reject funcy.primitives.EmptyType.__repr__: EMPTY, the only EmptyType, is a default marker that every function tests with `is` and never formats, prints or converts; the formatted values here are strs, ints, floats, exceptions, names, labels and client values
default reject funcy.decorators.Call.__str__, funcy.decorators.Call.__repr__: no Call is formatted or converted here, except "<Call %s>" % self in Call.__repr__ (decorators.py:85, accepted there); the formatted values are strs, numbers, exceptions, names and client arguments and results
default reject funcy.objects.wrap_prop.decorator.WrapperProp.__repr__: no other site formats a WrapperProp; nested wrap_prop explicitly accepts it at objects.py:40
#
# Conventions of this ledger (funcy.md has the judgements, J1 to J12):
# - Supported versions: Python 3.4 and later, CPython and PyPy (README.rst,
#   setup.py classifiers; no python_requires). Both lzip definitions and the
#   nullcontext fallback run (J2).
# - make_func(f) (funcmakers.py:10) returns: a callable f itself; for None
#   make_func.<lambda1>; for a str, bytes or regex re_finder.<lambda2>; for an
#   int or slice an operator.itemgetter; for a mapping its __getitem__ (an
#   abstract Mapping is opaque under B4, but docs/funcs.rst:179 gives compose
#   a concrete dict, adding its direct __getitem__ call); for a set its __contains__, named
#   <builtin>.set.__contains__ by the "set" of docs/extended_fns.rst (J3).
#   make_pred(f) is the same with bool for None and re_tester.<lambda1> for a
#   str. A direct call of the result has these callees; where library code
#   (map, filter, groupby, a factory) calls it back, only the package lambdas
#   are edges (bool, an itemgetter, set.__contains__ called back are not).
# - Roles (B2, J1): a callback parameter of one docs entry (a function and its
#   list or iterator flavour, documented together, e.g. map/lmap). Its package
#   values are those that the package's own code, the tests (tests/) and the
#   documented examples (docs/*.rst, README.rst) pass there. Client callables
#   (lambdas, whatever's _ expressions, builtins, bound methods, partials) are
#   opaque.
# - A lazy map, filter, filterfalse, takewhile, dropwhile, chain or groupby
#   that the package builds with a package callback calls it in the package
#   function that iterates it (list(), set(), next(), a factory, a for loop);
#   this ledger places the edge at that call (J4). README ruling12 also allows
#   a map/filter callback at construction when the same function consumes it.
#   The exception does not extend to groupby. A lazy object returned to the client
#   gives no package edge; where the documentation hands it back to another
#   funcy function, that function makes the call (J5).

